#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sin x}(\\cot x + \\sin^{2} x)dx &= \\int(\\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\cot x + \\frac{1}{\\sin x} \\cdot \\sin^{2} x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int(\\csc x \\cdot \\cot x + \\sin x)dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\csc x\\cot xdx + \\int\\sin xdx \\\\\n&=-\\csc x - \\cos x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\tan x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x\n$$\n\nto express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/3}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/3}\\sec x\\tan xdx \\\\\n&= \\left.\\sec x\\right|_{0}^{π/3} \\\\\n&= \\sec (\\frac{π}{3}) - \\sec (0) \\\\\n&= 2 - 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x$." :question/worked-solution "We use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}\n$$\n\nand rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}dx &= -\\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}u & + C \\\\ =-\\frac{1}{2}\\ln & \\cos 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}(\\cos 2x)^{-1} & + C \\\\ = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln & \\sec 2x\\end{vmatrix}+ C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "876f3e0e-cb6c-5f07-8744-addcb894df77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\cos x}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062046} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf39ac3-5e0d-5892-8dd5-a28a984c829d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\cos x} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062047} {:answer/id #uuid "0b71b3b4-3e34-5777-ad43-06a3298b30a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\cos x}} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062048} {:answer/id #uuid "2098db2c-30d0-529a-bdd6-cf1d42ce2b31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\tan x} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062049} {:answer/id #uuid "34085639-c720-5cf2-8d06-5a5309602155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{\\sin x} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf39ac3-5e0d-5892-8dd5-a28a984c829d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{\\cos x} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062047} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffc683e6-b935-577f-a851-fdb0eaef7e49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062045}] :question/id #uuid "1fabab33-132f-500c-992f-45cbf132915e" :question/math-academy-id "q-2622" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\tan x\\sqrt{\\cos x}dx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c74ab5e-6529-5eaa-85b3-a0782aadd834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\csc^{2} x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063161} {:answer/id #uuid "e2187799-969c-5da2-b0d4-3540ee6dfc84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\tan^{2} x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063162} {:answer/id #uuid "fea50b33-fd83-57f0-b032-99b9830582ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\tan x\\sec x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063163} {:answer/id #uuid "149d2ee5-0fcc-59e8-a748-a4c5ab9ef188" :answer/type 